From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 11:15:47 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7996: leave firmware CAC after a post-CSA CAC

A CSA to a DFS channel that still needs CAC programs the new channel
with the DFS switch reason and starts CAC in firmware, which mutes all
TX on the band. mac80211 runs this CAC after the switch and then
completes the switch without a chanctx change. Nothing tells the
firmware that the CAC ended, so the band stays muted: no beacons go
out and queued frames never complete.

Program the channel again when the switch completes and the phy is
still in CAC. cfg80211 has marked the channel available by then, so
the firmware gets a normal switch and leaves CAC. The chanctx chandef
is used rather than the link chandef, because a narrower BSS on a
shared context must not narrow the phy.

Based-on-work-by: StanleyYP Wang <StanleyYP.Wang@mediatek.com>
Based-on-work-by: Oskar Maroszek <oskar.maroszek@adtran.com>
Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
 mac80211.c    |    1 +
 mt7996/main.c |    5 ++++-
 2 files changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/mac80211.c
+++ b/mac80211.c
@@ -1134,6 +1134,7 @@ int mt76_set_channel(struct mt76_phy *ph
 
 	return ret;
 }
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(mt76_set_channel);
 
 int mt76_update_channel(struct mt76_phy *phy)
 {
--- a/mt7996/main.c
+++ b/mt7996/main.c
@@ -1224,7 +1224,10 @@ mt7996_post_channel_switch(struct ieee80
 out:
 	mutex_unlock(&dev->mt76.mutex);
 
-	return ret;
+	if (ret || phy->mt76->dfs_state != MT_DFS_STATE_CAC)
+		return ret;
+
+	return mt76_set_channel(phy->mt76, &phy->mt76->main_chandef, false);
 }
 
 static void
